{{gap:1}}
Walkers in parts of the Sahara, the Gobi and Western Australia have occasionally been startled by a low booming note rising straight from beneath their boots. Rather than a brief whistle of wind across a ridge, the sound resembles a distant propeller plane or bowed cello and can reverberate across a quiet valley for several minutes at a time.

{{gap:2}}
Long before physicists carried microphones into desert basins, travellers invented imaginative accounts for what they heard. Marco Polo wrote in the thirteenth century of unseen drums echoing across empty dunes, warning caravan guides never to lag behind lest the phantom voices lure them away from the safe track. Other legends blamed buried temple bells or hollow caverns hidden beneath the ridges.

{{gap:3}}
Despite its fame, fewer than forty dune fields worldwide are known to boom reliably. Laboratory studies show that singing sand requires an unusually strict combination of physical features: well-rounded quartz grains between 0.1 and 0.5 millimetres across, a dry outer layer resting over a slightly firmer base, and a leeward slope steep enough for a thin sheet of grains to slide smoothly.

{{gap:4}}
Once footsteps or a gust of wind trigger a shallow avalanche, millions of grains shear past one another down the slope. Because the grains are remarkably uniform in size, their tiny impacts fall into step instead of cancelling each other out, transforming the upper metre of dry sand into a natural loudspeaker that projects a steady low-frequency wave.

{{gap:5}}
Surprisingly, kicking harder down the slope makes the boom louder, not higher or lower. Pitch is governed almost entirely by grain diameter: finer sand vibrates faster to emit a higher hum, whereas coarser sand produces a deeper bass rumble—and rare dunes containing two distinct grain populations can even sing two separate musical notes simultaneously.

{{gap:6}}
Even the most reliable singing dune can fall mute overnight when the air turns damp. A microscopic film of morning dew acts like weak glue between neighbouring grains, forcing the sliding layer to break into silent, sluggish clumps rather than vibrating cleanly across the dry sand beneath.
